From: Lukas Klizas <lukas@klizas.lt>
Date: Thu, 28 Aug 2026 21:10:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] brcmfmac: detect and reload firmware that dies silently across suspend

BCM4364 firmware can lose all msgbuf ring state across S3 while still
ACKing the D3_INFORM handshake at suspend entry. Seen when the system
suspends shortly after a deauth (suspend without userspace network
quiesce: brcmf_cfg80211_suspend's brcmf_link_down + 500ms settle runs,
but the firmware is still processing the disconnect when D3 arrives).
On resume the device passes brcmf_pcie_pm_leave_D3()'s INTMASK check,
takes the hot-resume path, and every control message from then on times
out with -EIO. Nothing ever triggers recovery: a dead firmware sends no
FWHALT mailbox, so the device stays dead until reboot.

Two changes:

1. brcmf_pcie_pm_leave_D3(): treat an all-ones INTMASK readback as a
   dead device (MMIO read did not reach it), not a running one, and
   take the existing remove + re-probe path.

2. brcmf_cfg80211_resume(): probe the firmware with one cheap
   BRCMF_C_GET_VERSION query after every resume. Healthy firmware
   answers immediately; an unresponsive one hits the 2s ioctl timeout,
   and we schedule the existing bus_reset worker (same machinery as the
   debugfs "reset" file and FWHALT handling) to reload the firmware.
   brcmf_bus_schedule_reset() is un-static'd for this; it is preferred
   over brcmf_fw_crashed() to skip the coredump, which reads device
   memory over MMIO that a wedged BCM4364 may not tolerate.

Applies on top of 0001-brcmfmac-suspend-fix.patch (the leave_D3 hunk
context includes its d3_ack_timeout block).

Verified on MacBookPro16,1: 7 suspend/resume cycles (logind and direct
rtcwake -m mem, idle and under wlan0 traffic) with zero false-positive
resets, and the recovery worker verified end-to-end via the debugfs
reset file (firmware reload + reassociation in ~15s). The wedge itself
is a race and did not reproduce in 6 direct-suspend attempts, so the
timeout->reset trigger is verified by review, not in vivo.

Signed-off-by: Lukas Klizas <lukas@klizas.lt>
---
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/pcie.c	2026-08-28 21:01:15.972561116 +0300
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/pcie.c	2026-08-28 21:01:41.174880101 +0300
@@ -2674,6 +2674,7 @@
 	struct brcmf_pciedev_info *devinfo;
 	struct brcmf_bus *bus;
 	struct pci_dev *pdev;
+	u32 intmask;
 	int err;
 
 	brcmf_dbg(PCIE, "Enter\n");
@@ -2693,8 +2694,12 @@
 		goto cleanup;
 	}
 
-	/* Check if device is still up and running, if so we are ready */
-	if (brcmf_pcie_read_reg32(devinfo, devinfo->reginfo->intmask) != 0) {
+	/* Check if device is still up and running, if so we are ready.
+	 * All-ones means the read did not reach the device at all; treat
+	 * it like a powered-off device, not a running one.
+	 */
+	intmask = brcmf_pcie_read_reg32(devinfo, devinfo->reginfo->intmask);
+	if (intmask != 0 && intmask != 0xffffffff) {
 		brcmf_dbg(PCIE, "Try to wakeup device....\n");
 		if (brcmf_pcie_send_mb_data(devinfo, BRCMF_H2D_HOST_D0_INFORM))
 			goto cleanup;
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/core.c	2026-08-28 21:01:15.969441191 +0300
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/core.c	2026-08-28 21:01:43.337919055 +0300
@@ -1172,7 +1172,7 @@
  * teardown.  The remove path sets ->removing and drains the work while
  * holding bus_reset_lock, so a racing armer is either drained or skips it.
  */
-static void brcmf_bus_schedule_reset(struct brcmf_bus *bus_if)
+void brcmf_bus_schedule_reset(struct brcmf_bus *bus_if)
 {
 	mutex_lock(&bus_if->bus_reset_lock);
 	if (bus_if->drvr && bus_if->drvr->bus_reset.func && !bus_if->removing)
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/bus.h	2026-08-28 21:01:15.969439515 +0300
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/bus.h	2026-08-28 21:01:44.737944372 +0300
@@ -314,6 +314,7 @@
 void brcmf_dev_coredump(struct device *dev);
 /* Indication that firmware has halted or crashed */
 void brcmf_fw_crashed(struct device *dev);
+void brcmf_bus_schedule_reset(struct brcmf_bus *bus_if);
 
 /* Configure the "global" bus state used by upper layers */
 void brcmf_bus_change_state(struct brcmf_bus *bus, enum brcmf_bus_state state);
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/cfg80211.c	2026-08-28 21:01:15.969439515 +0300
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/cfg80211.c	2026-08-28 21:01:51.955076201 +0300
@@ -4168,6 +4168,7 @@
 	struct brcmf_cfg80211_info *cfg = wiphy_to_cfg(wiphy);
 	struct net_device *ndev = cfg_to_ndev(cfg);
 	struct brcmf_if *ifp = netdev_priv(ndev);
+	u32 ver;
 
 	brcmf_dbg(TRACE, "Enter\n");
 
@@ -4188,6 +4189,19 @@
 			cfg->wowl.nd_enabled = false;
 		}
 	}
+
+	/* Firmware can lose all msgbuf state across suspend yet still pass
+	 * the PCIe hot-resume register check (seen on BCM4364 suspended
+	 * shortly after a deauth): every control message then times out
+	 * until the firmware is reloaded. Probe it with one cheap query;
+	 * if it does not answer, schedule the bus reset to reload it
+	 * instead of leaving a dead device behind.
+	 */
+	if (brcmf_fil_cmd_int_get(ifp, BRCMF_C_GET_VERSION, &ver)) {
+		bphy_err(cfg->pub, "firmware unresponsive after resume, reloading\n");
+		brcmf_bus_schedule_reset(cfg->pub->bus_if);
+	}
+
 	return 0;
 }
 
